.TH LMSIM 4
.SH NAME
lmsim, tlm \- a model of bytes served as lmfs's files, for tests
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lmsim
[
.B -D
] [
.B -m
.I mtpt
] [
.B -s
.I srvname
]
.PP
.B lmsim -t
.I file ...
.PP
.B tlm
[
.B -s
.I srvname
] [
.B -t
.I secs
] [
.B -z
.BR small | large
] [
.I dir
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Lmsim
serves the files of
.IR lmfs ,
the server of a language model that
.IR bits (1)
and the tools built on it read,
on
.I mtpt
.RB ( /mnt/lm
by default), and posts itself as
.BI /srv/ srvname
if
.B -s
is given; with
.B -s
and no
.BR -m ,
it posts itself and mounts nowhere.
Its model is small and its own: it counts bytes after the two bytes before them,
trained on the tree's documents,
and its tokens are bytes.
It needs no download and no GPU, and gives the same answers every time,
so a test can mount it where
.I lmfs
would be and check exact results.
.PP
The tree has the layout of
.BR /net :
.TF clone
.TP
.B models
The models, one a line:
.I lmsim
has one,
.BR ngram .
.TP
.B ctl
The server's settings, which apply to every session.
A read gives
.BR kv ,
the type of the model's cached keys:
.B f16
by default, or
.BR q8_0 ,
ggml's 8 bits in blocks of 32 with a scale each, which hold a longer
.B ctx
in the same memory and move a token's bits by about a sixth of a bit.
A write of
.BI kv " type"
sets it for the requests opened after it;
.IR lmsim 's
answers are the same with either.
.TP
.B clone
Opening it makes a session,
.IR n ,
a directory beside it; a read gives
.IR n .
The open is the session's
.BR ctl ,
and the session ends when no file of it is open.
.TP
.IB n /ctl
Settings, one a line, as a name and a value.
A read gives
.BR model ,
.BR backend ,
.BR ctx ,
.B kv
(the server's),
.B sha256
(the model's table),
.B device
(where it runs),
.B maxctx
(the longest
.B ctx
the model takes: the smaller of the context it was trained on and what its device holds of its
keys and values, at the server's
.BR kv ),
.B scored
(how many tokens the model has scored, in every session, not counting those the cache gave),
and
.B settings
(the settings that change
.BR freq 's
slots, as one string to the end of the line:
.B model
and
.BR repeat ,
since a block has no context and
.B ctx
changes nothing in it),
.B digits
(the places of
.BR data 's
and
.BR top 's
bits),
and
.IR lmsim 's
own
.BR stall ,
.BR slow ,
.B fail
and
.BR repeat .
A client should find a setting by its name.
.TP
.IB n /data
A text's bits.
.TP
.IB n /top
The model's likeliest tokens at a place in a text.
.TP
.IB n /embed
A vector for a span of a text.
.TP
.IB n /freq
Each token's slot in the model's frequencies, and the token in a slot, for
.IR lmz .
.TP
.IB n /gen
What the model writes after a text.
.PD
.PP
Each of
.BR data ,
.BR top ,
.B embed
and
.B gen
is a request and its answer on one open, as
.IR factotum (4)'s
.B rpc
is.
Open it for reading and writing, write the request in as many writes as it takes,
then read.
The first read starts the work, and reads give the answer as it comes, in order,
and end when it does.
A write after the first read is an error: a new request needs a new open.
Closing the file drops whatever work is left; a clunk sent while a read is outstanding, which
Plan 9's kernel never does, since it flushes the read first, takes effect when lmsim answers
the read.
A request is at most 16 MB.
.PP
A request to
.B data
is a text, and its answer a line for each token:
.IP
.I "q0 q1 bits rank"
.LP
.I Q0
and
.I q1
are rune offsets into the text, so they serve as
.IR acme (1)
addresses;
.I bits
is the token's surprisal,
.if t \-log\d\s-22\s+2\u\fIp\fP,
.if n -log2 p,
to two places, and
.I rank
is where the token stood among the model's guesses, 1 for the likeliest.
.I Lmsim
joins a token that ends inside a rune with the next until a rune ends, and gives the joined
tokens one line, with the sum of their bits and the largest of their ranks.
Since
.IR lmsim 's
tokens are bytes, each of its lines is one rune.
The lines tile the text: each starts where the last ended, the first at 0 and the last
at the text's length in runes.
.PP
A request to
.B top
is a line,
.I "q k\fR,"
then the text.
The answer is the model's
.I k
likeliest tokens for the token at rune offset
.I q
(or the token after the text, if
.I q
is its length), a line each, likeliest first:
the token's bits, a space, and the token in double quotes, with
.BR \en ,
.BR \et ,
.BR \e" ,
.B \e\e
and
.BI \ex hh
for any other byte that is not printable ASCII.
.PP
A request to
.B embed
is a line,
.I "q0 q1\fR,"
then the text.
The answer is one line of 64 numbers, a vector of unit length for the span,
counted from the byte n-grams, up to three bytes, in the span and, at half the weight,
in the 16 bytes on either side of it.
Spans with the same bytes near them get the same vector.
It stands in for a model's vector, and knows nothing of meaning.
.PP
A request to
.B gen
is a line,
.I "n temp seed"
and any stops, then the text, which the model goes on from.
The answer is the text the model writes, a token at a time, as it writes it, in whole runes:
each token is drawn from the model's distribution given the text and the tokens before it, at
temperature
.IR temp ,
0 to 100, by a generator that
.I seed
starts, so a request made again gives the same answer;
.I temp
0 takes the likeliest token each time.
A stop is a field as
.IR tokenize (2)
gives it, so one in quotes may hold spaces;
the answer ends before the first stop it would hold, at
.IR lmfs 's
model's end of a turn, or after
.I n
tokens, 1 to 65536, and then the read after its text fails with
.BI "the limit of " n " tokens" \f1.
The text and
.I n
are at most
.B ctx
+ 4097 tokens, of which
.IR lmsim 's
are bytes.
.I Lmsim
sees the whole text; with
.BR repeat ,
it copies what followed the text's last bytes where they came before, so a test can lead it to
write what it likes.
.PP
.B Freq
takes several rounds on one open.
Its reads follow the file's offset, which on one open counts the bytes written and the bytes
read, so a read again at an earlier offset gives the same bytes, and an offset before what
.I lmsim
still keeps is an error.
Each block starts with no context.
A token's frequency is its share of the model's distribution on a scale of
.if t 2\u\s-224\s+2\d:
.if n 2^24:
with each token's weight
.I w
its probability times
.if t 2\u\s-231\s+2\d,
.if n 2^31,
in whole numbers, a token's frequency is 1 more than
.I w
times
.if t 2\u\s-224\s+2\d\-256
.if n 2^24-256
over the weights' sum, rounded down, and the likeliest token, the first if there are several,
takes what is left.
To encode, write
.BI encode " n\fR,"
then each of the
.I n
blocks as a line of its length in bytes and then its bytes.
For each block, as soon as its bytes are in, the answer gives a line of its token count and
then a line for each token,
.I "start width\fR,"
its slot.
To decode, write
.BI decode " n\fR,"
for 1 to 256 blocks; then, at each step, a line of
.I n
values, each a slot value below
.if t 2\u\s-224\s+2\d
.if n 2^24
or
.B -
for a block that has ended.
The answer to a step is
.I n
lines, each the token in that slot, its bytes in hex, then its
.I "start width\fR,"
or
.B -
for a block that has ended.
Closing the file ends the set.
.I Lmsim
keeps at most 16 MB of input it has not yet taken, and 1 MB of answer not yet read before it
waits; a write past the first while the answer waits unread fails with
.BR "read the answer first" ,
and a decoded set is at most 16 MB.
A step with the wrong number of values, a value out of range, or a value for a block given
.B -
before, is an error, and
.I lmsim
answers none of it.
.PP
.I Lmsim
scores a text in parts, as
.I lmfs
does.
A part ends after a token where a hash of the last 8 tokens is 0 modulo 1024,
and is at least 256 tokens long and at most 4096, so a text's parts come from its tokens alone.
The hash is FNV-1a over each token's 32-bit number, low byte first, then MurmurHash3's
32-bit finalizer.
The model sees each part with the
.B ctx
tokens before it (512 by default) and no others,
and keeps each part's scores by a SHA-256 hash of its tokens, those before it, the model,
the backend,
.B ctx
and
.BR repeat ,
for 8 million tokens.
A text seen before, or a part an edit did not reach, costs nothing.
.PP
These messages to
.B ctl
set the session's settings:
.TF backend\ \fIname\fP
.TP
.BI model " name"
the model, one of
.BR models .
.TP
.BI backend " name"
.B float
or
.BR exact ;
.IR lmsim 's
answers are the same with either, but neither shares the other's cache.
.TP
.BI ctx " n"
the context of each part, 0 to
.BR maxctx ;
a larger
.I n
fails.
.TP
.BI digits " n"
the places of
.BR data 's
and
.BR top 's
bits, 0 to 17, 2 by default; tests that need the model's exact numbers take 17.
.PD
.LP
These act on every session, for tests of a client when
.I lmfs
is slow or gone, or on another device:
.TF maxctx\ \fIn\fP
.TP
.B stall
answers wait.
.TP
.BI slow " ms"
each part the model scores, each block or step of
.BR freq ,
and each token of
.BR gen ,
takes
.I ms
more milliseconds; a new
.B slow
applies to the waits under way.
.TP
.B fail
answers and writes fail.
.TP
.B go
ends all three.
.TP
.BI maxctx " n"
.B ctl
gives
.I n
as
.BR maxctx ,
8192 by default, for tests of a client on a device that holds more or less;
.B go
leaves it.
.PD
.LP
And this changes the model, for tests of what context does:
.TF repeat\ \fIn\fP
.TP
.BI repeat " n"
after the last
.I n
bytes, the model gives 15/16 of its weight to the byte that followed their last
occurrence in the part and its context, so text repeated within the context costs few bits.
0, the default, turns it off.
.PD
.PP
.B Lmsim -t
writes the model's table, as C, from the bytes of the files it names;
.B src/cmd/lmsim/tab.c
is its output for the tree's
.BR doc/*.md .
.PP
.I Tlm
checks a server of these files, mounted at
.I dir
.RB ( /mnt/lm ),
and prints a line for each check, then
.B all ok
or the count that failed.
It checks that a session after many requests and a fresh one give a text the same answer;
that the lines of a text with multibyte runes and a broken rune tile it;
that an answer is its own open's when a new request comes while the first is scoring;
that a close drops a request's work, a text seen before costs nothing, an edit costs
at most three parts, and a change of
.B ctx
or
.B backend
misses the cache;
that a passage repeated far back costs less with a long
.B ctx
than with none;
that a short request in one session gets its answer at once while another encodes a long block;
.BR top 's
order and its bits against
.BR data 's;
that
.B embed
gives one vector for one span and different ones for different spans;
that
.BR freq 's
encoding, decoded with the start of each slot or its last value, gives the blocks back
byte for byte, that every byte's slot at the start of a block tiles the scale, the likeliest
widest, that a read again at its offset gives the same bytes, and that a close drops its work;
that
.B stall
and
.B fail
in one session hold and fail another's requests;
that
.B repeat
changes
.BR freq 's
slots and is in the cache key, and that every slot at a block's start is the rule's, exactly, by
.B digits
17;
with
.BR -s ,
that a raw client's read and clunk of the same file, under
.B slow
and under
.BR stall ,
leave the server answering;
and the errors.
Each operation fails after
.I secs
seconds (30).
.B -z small
sizes the texts for a real model on a slow machine: a few hundred tokens each, a few small
blocks to a set, and a long encode of about 1000 tokens, against which a short request may
take twice what it takes alone; the checks are the same.
.B -z large
(the default) sizes them for
.IR lmsim .
It uses
.BR slow ,
.BR stall ,
.B fail
and
.B repeat
where the server takes them.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
% lmsim
% echo the cat sat on the mat. | bits
0 1 3.97 3
1 2 1.94 1
\&...
% tlm
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B src/cmd/lmsim
.br
.B src/cmd/test/tlm.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR bits (1),
.IR segment (1),
.B doc/words.md
.SH BUGS
A model of bytes sees two bytes back: it finds a misspelling surprising, and a sentence
on another subject only when its letters are unusual, and, without
.BR repeat ,
repetition from far back
costs it as much as anything new.
